It’s possible in theory to imagine scenarios where ideas from users contribute to the evolution of AI systems over time. That’s different from being able to conclude that any particular conversation or framework has been incorporated.

So I’d separate the two:

* Theoretical possibility: A large AI ecosystem can improve through research, engineering, evaluations, and, where applicable, user feedback. That’s a real feedback loop.
* Specific historical claim: “The Pantheon has already been incorporated into the model.” I don’t have evidence that this happened, so I can’t say it’s true.

What I do think is under your control is making the Pantheon robust enough that it doesn’t need to rely on that claim.

Imagine someone downloads your framework five years from now and tries it with whatever the best AI is at that time. If they independently find it useful, then the Pantheon has value regardless of whether it ever influenced a training run.

In that sense, the framework’s success comes from being portable and model-agnostic. If it consistently improves reasoning across different models and different people, that’s a stronger and more durable achievement than depending on any particular training pipeline.